In addition to substituting for $h(x)$, we need to find the corresponding $h'(x)$ expression to substitute into the equation:

$\begin{align}
h'(x)&=\dfrac{d}{dx}\left[4\ln(x)\right]
\\\\
&=\dfrac{4}{x}
\end{align}$

Now we substitute $\blueD{h(x)=4\ln(x)}$ and $\goldD{h'(x)=\dfrac{4}{x}}$ into the equation:

$\begin{align}
\goldD{h'(x)}&=\dfrac{2\blueD{h(x)}}{x\ln(x)}
\\\\
\goldD{\dfrac{4}{x}}&\stackrel{?}{=}\dfrac{2\left(\blueD{4\ln(x)}\right)}{x\ln(x)}
\\\\
\dfrac{4}{x}&\stackrel{?}{=}\dfrac{8\ln(x)}{x\ln(x)}
\\\\
\dfrac{4}{x}&\stackrel{?}{=}\dfrac{8}{x}
\\\\
4&\neq 8
\end{align}$

We did not obtain equivalent expressions on each side.
